          "case_name": "Deegan v. Jefferson County",
          "citation": "188 Wis. 2d 544, 525 N.W.2d 149 (Ct. App. 1994)",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of Wisconsin",
          "year": 1994,
          "date_decided": "1994-10-27",
          "holding": "Wisconsin recognizes no damages claim against caseworkers for intentional interference with 'family unity,' and in any event county social workers are immune for discretionary acts; conclusory allegations of malice do not pierce that immunity.",
          "relevance": "A screen-out or closure decision is discretionary and not actionable in tort absent a ministerial duty.",
          "quote": "public officers and employees enjoy immunity from suit for discretionary actions undertaken in the scope of their public employment",
